WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:06.140
Malcolm Grant
Chairman, NHS England

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:07.140
Preamble
Good morning ladies and gentlemen and welcome

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.140
to this meeting of the Board of NHS England.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.510
We are meeting this morning in public in Leeds,
and on behalf of the Board I would like very

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:16.059
much to welcome those of you who have joined
us in the meeting room this morning.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:21.339
It is a meeting in public rather than a public
meeting; in other words, we are this morning

00:00:21.339 --> 00:00:27.169
transacting quite a volume of business, as
you will see from our agenda, and we will

00:00:27.169 --> 00:00:37.140
be spending quite an amount of time on some
really important issues.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:44.399
At the end of the meeting we will go to a
private session, but I would like to say informally

00:00:44.399 --> 00:00:50.269
that members of the Board are very happy to
talk to individuals in between the two meetings.

00:00:50.269 --> 00:00:55.679
The private meeting this afternoon will be
addressing a number of issues which are commercial

00:00:55.679 --> 00:00:59.750
in confidence and which we are not able to
take in the public meeting.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:07.250
I should say the reason of doing this stuff
in public is that we on NHS England get it:

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:13.000
that we have an extraordinarily serious and
honourable responsibility, which is to ensure

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:21.130
that decisions are taken about the care that
is provided by the NHS and commissioned through

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:28.710
the funds which reside with us, are taken
in public, openly and transparently.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:32.520
I am therefore insisting on every occasion
that the business brought to the private meeting

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:37.750
has a good, substantial reason for it being
taken privately.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:44.500
We also meet as a Board informally from time
to time, around what we call strategy or development

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:45.500
sessions.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:52.430
Some of the early work of business that comes
formally to the Board is undertaken by a combination

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:57.000
of executive members, non-executive members
around the table and development sessions.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:04.140
For example, yesterday, we spent the morning
thinking about the future of the potential

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:13.970
impact of technology in the provision of healthcare
within England, not only looking at IT and

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:17.629
data, but other technologies which may come
onto the market.

00:02:17.629 --> 00:02:25.459
In the afternoon we will look at genetics
and genomics, to try to understand how these

00:02:25.459 --> 00:02:31.700
played a role in not only the changes that
we might expect within healthcare globally,

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:37.659
but also the Five-Year Forward View that we
published recently and which we will return

00:02:37.659 --> 00:02:39.660
to this morning.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:47.110
So in that context, the items that we will
be attending to this morning are items of

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:52.959
business that we believe absolutely do need
to be taken and considered in public.

00:02:52.959 --> 00:02:55.800
Agenda
A note on the agenda: you will see at the

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:02.780
end of the agenda, under item 12, there are
several items of Board Committee feedback

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:10.110
that are starred, the purpose of which is
to invite any member of the Board to un-star

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:16.060
and to invite discussion on those items, otherwise
we will take them as reports to the Board

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:17.250
that are read.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:23.760
I have a notice so far of a request to un-star
the item relating to quality and clinical

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.740
risks; Sir Cyril Chantler will be joining
us at the table later this morning when he

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:32.630
arrives, and I know he's very keen to speak
about to paper.

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:36.460
So, I have opened the Board meeting formally.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:41.630
I would like to invite members of the Board
to declare any interests in any of the business

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:45.099
under consideration, if they have any?

00:03:45.099 --> 00:03:47.910
I have no declarations, thank you.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:56.519
I have apologies from Lord Adebowale; he has
written to me, and I have circulated his note

00:03:56.519 --> 00:04:00.629
relating to items on the agenda to all members
of the Board, and I have invited those who

00:04:00.629 --> 00:04:08.319
will be presenting on those items to reflect
on Victor's comments in their presentations.

00:04:08.319 --> 00:04:11.709
Safety
One final bit of business is to say that there

00:04:11.709 --> 00:04:21.100
is no fire alarm tests scheduled for the course
of the meeting, and if the alarms do sound

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:24.190
our team will escort you to a safe place.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.940
Minutes of the last meeting
Well, thank you very much.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:30.880
Then may I move into the business of the meeting.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:36.680
We have the minutes of the last meeting; are
there any issues on the minutes?

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:37.680
No, there are not.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.300
I do not have notice of any matters arising,
so therefore we will take the minutes and

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:44.910
pass on.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:49.860
We have now the Chief Executive's report and
I'll invite Simon Stephens to introduce it.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:50.860
Chief Executive’s report
Simon Stevens

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:51.860
Chief Executive, NHS England

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:52.860
Preamble
Thank you, Malcom.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.580
I am going to be quite brief, because most
of the items are covered at greater length

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:01.590
elsewhere on the agenda, but I just wanted
to pick out three things.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:03.430
Five-year forward view
Publication

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:10.340
Firstly, to note the publication of the NHS
Five-Year Forward View on 23rd October which,

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:17.720
as the Board will remember following our conversation
at the last meeting, was a joint production

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:23.290
from the six national leadership bodies in
the National Health Service, Monitor, TDA,

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:28.130
Care Quality Commission, Health Education,
Public Health England and NHS England.

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:33.640
This was all six of us setting out a shared
point of view and a set of actions that we

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:39.580
commit to take in order to help work with
local partners and broader stakeholders on

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:44.740
three big questions: why does healthcare in
this country need to change?

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:46.500
What will it look like when we have done so?

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:48.390
And how are we going to get there?

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:53.780
I think we were all gratified by the support
and the reception that the Five-Year Forward

00:05:53.780 --> 00:06:00.700
View received, which in part was precisely
because it does represent a very significant

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:06.200
latent consensus on a number of these big-ticket
questions, which is sometimes concealed either

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:14.520
by the immediate issues in front of us or
by some other debates that are sometimes obscure.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:19.120
People do understand why, although the health
service has done very well last 15 years or

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:24.780
the last five years, doing more of the same
is unlikely to be sustainable over the next

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:25.780
five.

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:32.480
And so we are going to have to make some big
changes on prevention and demand, some big

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:39.430
changes on how care is organised and some
big changes on how to drive efficiency and

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:40.800
investment in the system.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:46.670
So it provides a good route map for a lot
of work that is now required, both to continue

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:52.860
the joint national working that exemplified
this effort, but also to ensure that we have

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:58.680
a proper national-local dialogue and that
we continue to properly engage with broader

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:03.960
patient groups, citizens groups, local government
and all of the partners who help shaped this

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:06.260
in the first place.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:11.950
Regional meeting
David Bennett, David Flory and I have conducted

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:16.720
four regional meetings around the country
within the last ten days or so for the chief

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:24.410
executives of our provider organisations,
CCGs and local authorities to begin dialogue

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:29.950
about how to now advance the priorities set
out in the Forward View.

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:35.590
We will obviously be doing further work between
us and coming back to this Board on what that

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:36.590
will look like.

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:41.130
We want to make some pretty substantial progress
on this by spring.

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:47.130
Elements of it will be embedded in the shared
planning guidance that we will be issuing

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:52.660
for 2015–16, so there are a set of immediate
things that we want to get on with as well

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:56.700
as some long-term choices and positioning
that is now required.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:58.900
Waiting time
Winter period

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:01.770
So, that’s the Forward View.

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:07.270
For here and now, as you know and can see
and will discuss in the NHS Performance Report,

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:13.550
huge mobilisation of efforts across the National
Health Service to ensure that we get waiting

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:16.170
time back on track.

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:22.240
Hospitals in particular are engaged in a major
effort to treat long waiters by the end of

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:28.180
November, to give themselves headroom for
the winter period.

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:36.370
Barbara will describe significant work right
across the country on managing A&amp;E pressures,

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:44.630
ambulance pressures, particularly in combination
with TDA and Monitor, ensuring that the Health

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:49.810
Service is pulling out all the stops to try
and be sure that our patients get the care

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:52.930
they are entitled to over this winter period.

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:56.740
Mental health
You will also see that we published for the

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:01.150
first time mental health access standards
which will begin to take effect from next

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:02.150
year.

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:05.840
We think these are very important, both at
the severe and enduring mental health end

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:12.770
of the spectrum and also for our anxiety and
depression services, and that is just the

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:15.890
first step in a longer journey that we want
to take.

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:20.750
Also we recognise that we are going need to
continue to see much more progress on child

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:25.070
and adolescent mental health services, as
recently underlined by an important report

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:28.100
by the Health Select Committee earlier this
week.

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:30.540
Contingency planning
Ebola

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:35.150
The third and final set of things I would
just like to draw attention to would be some

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:41.210
contingency planning which is going on in
respect of both of the industrial relations

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:46.420
dispute that is going on across the Health
Service and in particular planning and preparedness

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:47.420
for Ebola.

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:53.890
I might, Malcom, just ask Barbara to briefly
update the board on the Ebola preparedness,

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:59.670
where again, huge cross-sectional action,
recognising that the Department of Health

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:04.170
and Public Health England have leadership
responsibilities here alongside us.

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:11.920
Barbara Hakin: The Board will be aware of
NHS England’s specific role in contingency

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:15.540
and planning for unusual episodes for emergencies.

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:24.670
Industrial action is one, and there is already
a lot of activity to ensure that we are prepared

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:30.390
for the first round of industrial action and
that a lot of continuing activity to make

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:35.790
sure that particularly our ambulance services
are supported and ready if there is any future

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:36.790
industrial action.

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:43.430
But there is a specific focus at the moment
on Ebola, and because this is deemed as preparedness

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:48.690
for an emergency then NHS England’s role
changes and we are responsible for ensuring

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:56.740
that the NHS is completely prepared for all
aspects of dealing with this serious illness.

00:10:56.740 --> 00:11:01.640
As part of that, we take a different role.

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:05.870
Under normal circumstances when we are dealing
with routine care for patients, we commission

00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:10.780
the care, we describe the services and we
are responsible for ensuring that the services

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:17.550
that we don't have direct command or control
of are provided by a provider organisation

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:20.360
who are overseen by Monitor and the NHS TDA.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:26.800
That changes when we have emergency planning,
and in terms of Ebola NHS England are in a

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:30.870
position, should it be necessary – and of
course the trusts and the provider organisations

00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:37.380
always comply and are very keen to support
us on this – but we do have the ability

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:40.350
to direct and therefore communicate directly
with them.

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:45.460
Over the last period I have been communicating
directly with trust chief executives about

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:47.260
this issue, which I wouldn’t normally do
on others.

00:11:47.260 --> 00:11:50.520
Six pillars of support
So, the overarching responsibility under the

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:56.090
direction of Cobra is managed by the Department
of Health, but you can see that we have got

00:11:56.090 --> 00:12:00.350
a very significant role working very closely
with Public Health England and other partners.

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:05.800
There is a great deal of activity, but there
are six things particularly that other programme

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:09.560
planners put together that the NHS needs to
do in terms of supporting.

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:12.040
First of all, there is some support in West
Africa.

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:16.790
So a lot of our staff are going out to West
Africa to support there, give clinical advice,

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:21.240
anything else we need to do to help control
the disease there.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:25.910
The second, and we already had one instance
of it, is repatriation, if someone needs to

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:30.470
be brought back from elsewhere.

00:12:30.470 --> 00:12:35.260
I think we had an excellent result: they are
all free, we have the two very specialist

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:41.110
beds at the moment and it was a great relief
when an individual was returned, looked after

00:12:41.110 --> 00:12:43.930
in one of those beds and is now well again.

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:48.770
The third is screening at entry ports, and
whilst Public Health England and the Department

00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:53.542
of Health are overseeing the actual screening,
should screening result in the individual

00:12:53.542 --> 00:12:56.490
who is suspected of having Ebola then it is
entirely to the NHS.

00:12:56.490 --> 00:13:01.350
It is the response the NHS makes in ensuring
that the patient is properly dealt with until

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:05.990
we determine whether or not they do have Ebola.

00:13:05.990 --> 00:13:10.920
Another big part of the work is the awareness
and preparedness of the staff.

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:14.330
I think this is the one where we put in the
most effort, and there is still more work

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:15.330
to do.

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:19.800
We need to ensure that all first-point-of-contact
health staff understand what questions they

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:20.800
should be asking.

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:26.640
If there might be a suspicion that an individual
could have Ebola, we need to be sure that

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:32.910
they are aware what precautions they need
to take if the suspicion turns into significant

00:13:32.910 --> 00:13:36.110
assumption that the patient might be effective.

00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:41.231
So we have got a major communications exercise
with GP practices and every provider organisation

00:13:41.231 --> 00:13:42.231
to do that.

00:13:42.231 --> 00:13:46.570
We then move on to the fifth, which is the
handling of a suspected case.

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:51.340
So, what we do with individuals when we think
it's really likely that they have Ebola?

00:13:51.340 --> 00:13:54.940
How are we with the full treatment rather
than just that initial contact?

00:13:54.940 --> 00:14:00.580
And then finally, dealing with patients who
are diagnosed as having Ebola, and a major

00:14:00.580 --> 00:14:04.950
part of the work for us is the resilience
of the system if the numbers increase.

00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:08.590
At the moment we have had no cases diagnosed
in this country.

00:14:08.590 --> 00:14:13.620
We hope that that continues, but clearly the
NHS has to be in a position to deal with however

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:19.760
many patients we need to, and the plans to
open extra beds both at the Royal Free and

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:24.290
across other parts of the NHS in England to
treat patients who have Ebola is a critical

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:26.330
part of the workstream.

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:35.250
Just to assure the Board, we are doing a lot
of awareness training and we are also assuring

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:39.380
ourselves that those six passes of work are
being put into place.

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:44.650
We have written to all the provider organisations
to ask that they assure us that their staff

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:49.980
are fully briefed and have the right equipment
and would know how to use that equipment,

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:56.779
and we are doing some spot-checks to make
sure that at A&amp;E departments and GP surgeries

00:14:56.779 --> 00:14:59.839
the right information is available to staff.

00:14:59.839 --> 00:15:03.190
Malcolm Grant: Thank you Simon.

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:11.750
I know that the Board would wish me to express
our thanks and congratulations to all of those

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:14.660
who were involved in the preparation of the
Forward View.

00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:19.230
We are going to come back to it this morning,
but at this stage of the meeting we recognise

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:25.100
that this is an extraordinary collective effort
which has involved not only a large number

00:15:25.100 --> 00:15:29.050
of people within NHS England, but of course
in our partner NHS organisations.

00:15:29.050 --> 00:15:35.040
To have produced a report of such clarity
with such convergence of opinion, and to have

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:40.800
found such support amongst patient organisations
and think-tanks and other groups who can share

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:46.100
with us the strength of view as to how the
NHS needs to transform over the next five

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:49.610
years, has been a very significant achievement.

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:54.740
As we come back to it later this morning you
will hear from the Board various commitments

00:15:54.740 --> 00:16:02.241
to how we now drive you and the executive
team very hard forward for delivery but I

00:16:02.241 --> 00:16:05.550
would like formally to put on record our thanks
to the team and you particularly for your

00:16:05.550 --> 00:16:06.550
leadership.

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:07.550
Moira Gibb: I echo that, Malcolm.

00:16:07.550 --> 00:16:16.490
I just wanted to suggest that the Board puts
on record our appreciation for all the staff

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:18.930
involved in preparation for Ebola.

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:28.880
It is on top of very demanding jobs in a way,
and it is incredibly important for the country

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:34.810
that we should express our sincere thanks.

